description:
Goal: produce a small, opinionated set of cross-language starter templates that show how to wire Redwood SDKs for three common surfaces: Next.js (TypeScript), FastAPI (Python), Go net/http server + a thin CLI. Emphasis is on: streaming ergonomics (client & server), resilient retry strategies that preserve idempotency, graceful cancellation (context/AbortSignal), and cookbook snippets that illustrate token-level metrics and cost-aware batching.

What success looks like:
- 4 runnable examples (Next.js, FastAPI, Go server, CLI) with README walkthroughs and single-command local run.
- Shared guidance in each example for: retry policy, streaming backpressure, prompt/response shaping, and local mocking.
- One consolidated PR per language adding examples to the SDK repo and a single umbrella PR in docs for linking.

Decisions / design notes:
- Retry policy: default exponential backoff with jitter, maximum 3 retries, idempotency tagging in SDK calls for non-streaming endpoints. Streaming endpoints default to no automatic retry; instead provide manual resume hooks documented in examples.
- Streaming ergonomics: expose both an async-iterator style and a callback/handler style in TS/py to accommodate frameworks (React Server Actions vs. FastAPI background tasks). In Go, use context for cancellation and a small utility that converts streaming frames to channel values.
- Cost instrumentation: examples show token-level accounting (request tokens, response tokens) and a simple local dashboard (JSON output to stdout) for demonstration.

Example snippets (copyable):

TypeScript (Next.js edge route streaming consumer):

const res = await fetch('/api/gen', { method: 'POST' });
const reader = res.body!.getReader();
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
let partial = '';
while (true) {
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  partial += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });
  // call setState or append to DOM chunk-by-chunk
}

Python (FastAPI streaming endpoint with cancellation):

from fastapi import FastAPI, Request
app = FastAPI()
@api.post('/gen')
async def gen(request: Request):
    body = await request.json()
    cancel_event = request.state._client_cancel if hasattr(request.state, '_client_cancel') else None
    async for chunk in redwood_client.stream_generate(body):
        if cancel_event and cancel_event.is_set():
            break
        yield chunk

Go (net/http server: context cancellation + channel converter):

func streamHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
  ctx := r.Context()
  frames := make(chan string)
  go func(){
    defer close(frames)
    for f := range sdk.Stream(ctx, payload) {
      frames <- f
    }
  }()
  flusher, _ := w.(http.Flusher)
  for {
    select {
    case <-ctx.Done():
      return
    case f, ok := <-frames:
      if !ok { return }
      fmt.Fprintf(w, "%s", f)
      flusher.Flush()
    }
  }
}

CLI (resumable tail mode with manual resume token):

# run: rwd-cli gen --prompt "..." --resume-token-file /tmp/token.json

Behavior notes:
- CLI stores last-acknowledged resume token; users can pass it back to resume partial generations.
- For interactive shells, we provide sane line-buffering and a progress bar.

Links to artifacts (drafts):
- Design doc: https://confluence.redwood.ai/display/ENG/SDK+Orchestra+starter+bridge
- Example repo draft PRs: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk-examples/pull/112 (TS/Next), https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk-examples/pull/113 (Python/FastAPI), https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk-examples/pull/114 (Go + CLI)

Security: streaming endpoints documented with guidance to not log full responses by default and to use customer-managed KMS for keys when running private examples.
